WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.970
Cells are the fundamental unit of life.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.540
There are over 37 trillion
of them in the human body,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.300
100 times more than the number
of stars in the Milky Way.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.490
So each of the cells in our tissues

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.690
fulfills a different type of role,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.560
but together they like make
this beautiful symphony

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.480
that lets the tissue maintain itself

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.500
and then lets our organs
do their functions

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.443
and eventually our entire body.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.030
Over the past 300 years,

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.860
we've learned what cells are made of,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.490
how they function and
divide into new cells.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.390
But there's still a lot we don't know.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.470
How many different cell types
are there in the human body?

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.330
How do different cell types work together?

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.253
And how do changes in
cells cause diseases?

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.370
About 2,000 researchers from
over 70 different countries

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.260
are trying to answer these questions

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.220
by building a Human Cell Atlas,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.193
a complete map of all the
cell types in our body.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.990
What we envision is that
the Human Cell Atlas

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.500
will be a foundational reference
for biomedical research

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.843
in many, many areas.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.650
And it tells us something about ourselves,

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.550
about what our bodies are made of,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.470
and also going to help us and many others

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.499
over time develop new
medicines for patients.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.500
So we like to think about
the final atlas fondly

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.669
as a Google Map of the human body.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.307
You could say, well, I want
to understand the cells

00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:41.367
in the nose, or the
cells inside your mouth,

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.037
or the cells of the skin,

00:01:43.037 --> 00:01:45.490
or the cells of this
particular region in the brain.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.190
And you could drill into that region.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.910
First you could look at coarse resolution.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.950
You would know how the tissue is ordered,

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.160
and then you could go in
finer and finer resolutions

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.650
all the way to the level
of individual cells.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.000
Aviv Regev is the co-chair
of Human Cell Atlas,

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.880
a 10-year endeavor to
discover new cell types

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.583
as well as map them in detail.

00:02:05.001 --> 00:02:07.230
The other way that we like
to think about the atlas

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.780
is what we call the
periodic table of the cells.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.010
If you think about the
periodic table of the elements,

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.260
it's not just a description
of the elements.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.790
It's also a theory of the elements.

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.150
You know, Mendeleev was able to predict

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.670
that elements would exist

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.650
before they were actually
empirically found.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.810
And one of the things
that we hope will happen

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.050
with this atlas is that we will learn

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.473
how to better predict
cells and their behaviors.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.710
In 1664, around 200 years before Mendeleev

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.900
and his periodic table,
English scientist Robert Hooke

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.800
discovered the existence of cells

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.150
when he put a piece of
cork under a microscope.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.220
Ever since then, microscopy
has played an important role

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.683
in studying cell structure and function.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.570
By looking at cells under a microscope

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:54.880
and studying their reactions

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.740
with chemical stains,
which make them visible,

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.760
scientists identified about 300 cell types

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:01.813
in the human body.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.800
But cells which look
similar under a microscope

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.050
can sometimes turn out to
be chemically different.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:13.110
And so our knowledge has
been limited until now.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.990
Something happened a few years ago,

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.750
which was a major technological advance

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.630
that allows us to look
at the molecular content

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:22.440
of the individual cells

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.200
through their RNA molecules in particular.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.960
And we call this single-cell genomics.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.550
In the past, we take say a piece of tissue

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.150
that would have many different
kinds of cells in it.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:34.730
And we would put it basically

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.860
through the lab equivalent of a blender.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.730
And so if you think of every cell

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:41.980
as a different piece of fruit,

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.410
there's blueberries and strawberries

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.690
and raspberries and kiwis and so on,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.810
then what you get as a result
of that is very similar

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:48.700
to a fruit smoothie.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.590
It's a blend of all of
the molecular contents

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:52.423
of all of those cells

00:03:52.423 --> 00:03:54.003
and you get to measure the average.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:57.440
That is not a great way
by which to discover

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.420
what are the individual cells.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.810
What single-cell genomics allows us to do

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.010
is look at every individual cell

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.570
in the molecules within it separately.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.060
And so this gives us
basically the equivalent

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:10.350
of a fruit salad.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.180
Now you can see each
individual piece of strawberry

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.823
and blueberry and kiwi and raspberry.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:21.360
Where the workflow starts is
with acquiring the tissue.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.460
And so in that case there are biopsies

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.320
from deceased transplant donor tissue.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.180
The biopsy tissue is broken down

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.460
into single whole cells.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.420
These individual cells are loaded

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.290
onto a microfluidic droplet robot,

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.160
which carries out the chemical reactions

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.940
needed to prepare them for sequencing.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:43.033
We're measuring which genes
are switched on in each cell.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.770
To understand why single-cell

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.490
sequencing is important,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.160
we need to understand a little
bit about how cells work.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.370
Our genome, which is made of DNA,

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.620
is the instruction manual
for the cells in our body.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.300
Within this genome are
thousands of different genes,

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.640
which each code a different protein.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.530
These proteins are made
using a chemical called RNA.

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.600
By using single-cell sequencing,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.520
if you can identify which genes

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.490
each individual cell is using,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.563
you can tell what sort of cell it is.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.863
But there's one problem.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.550
We have about 25,000
different genes in our genome.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.670
And with single-cell genomics,

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.990
we can measure several thousand per cell.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.120
And so what the technology is telling us

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.870
is in each single cell
which specific subset

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.570
of 2,000 or 3,000 genes is
switched on in that single cell

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.070
out of the 25,000 possible.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.820
For every single cell, you
have several thousand genes

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.320
expressed and you can have
hundreds of thousands of cells.

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.830
So the data matrix is
hundreds of thousands

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.210
multiplied by thousands of data points.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.180
So we really are talking
about sizes of data

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:58.130
that are getting close to astronomical.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.260
With the help of machine learning

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:03.780
and artificial intelligence,

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.050
these huge amounts of data
can be processed and analyzed,

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.830
eventually leading to the
discovery of new cell types.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.460
The Human Cell Atlas community
has been able to reveal

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:18.350
dozens, maybe now up to even a hundred,

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.270
different new cell types

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.530
across different tissues of the body.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.349
So for example, several years ago

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:26.182
together with my colleagues,

00:06:26.182 --> 00:06:28.270
we did a study of the airways in the lungs

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.960
and we discovered a new cell type,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.750
which is very rare and
that nobody knew existed.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.420
It was literally not there
until we discovered it.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:36.670
Of course it was in our lungs,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.410
but it was not in our knowledge.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.850
And that cell type that
we call the ionocyte

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:43.440
ended up being especially exciting for us.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.370
Because it uses, it expresses very highly

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:50.360
the gene known as CFTR, or
the cystic fibrosis gene.

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.810
And until we discovered
this very rare cell type,

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.190
scientists actually assumed that this gene

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:57.910
is used by other cells in
the lung and the airways.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:00.470
And it turns out that it
is not used by those cells.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.330
It is used by these super rare cells

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:03.630
that we didn't know existed.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.840
Cystic fibrosis is a
fatal hereditary disease

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.103
that affects the lungs
and digestive system.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.870
The discovery of this new cell type

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.563
could help diagnose and treat the disease.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.420
Our hope is that this would allow us

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.310
to also understand disease better.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.560
Because what happens in
disease is that cells

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:24.170
all of a sudden misbehave.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:25.003
They don't do the things
that they're supposed to do.

00:07:25.003 --> 00:07:25.836
And what we want to develop

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:28.693
are the right kinds of medicines of course

00:07:28.693 --> 00:07:29.526
that would bring them back
to their native state.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.880
And we hope that the atlas
would be a foundational

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.900
reference and resource like
the human genome has been

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.010
in order to help
scientists both understand

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.516
basic biology and understand disease.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.270
Individual cell types identified

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.110
using single-cell sequencing

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:50.630
can then be located within tissue samples

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.910
to make a detailed 3D map.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.000
So far over 39 million
cells have been analyzed,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.450
covering specific organs, such
as the brain, skin and lungs.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.770
But to make a complete cell
atlas covering every tissue,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:07.090
organ and system in the human body,

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.150
billions more cells need to be analyzed.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:13.200
It is a crazy quest and
it's ridiculously ambitious,

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.890
but we have these little
milestones along the way.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:20.360
And each time we discover something new,

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.890
there's an incredible
excitement and thrill.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.883
And that keeps us going as well.

